    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The best overall roofing material for most Utah homes is Class 4 impact-resistant architectural asphalt shingles, while standing-seam metal roofing is the top choice for long-term durability and heavy snow areas.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Class 4 impact-resistant architectural asphalt shingles"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Utah's climate presents intense summer heat, high-altitude UV rays, freezing winters, heavy snow loads, and spring winds or hail. Different materials handle these conditions in specific ways:",
        "reference_indexes": [
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "1. Architectural Asphalt Shingles (Best All-Around Value)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan: 28\u201335 years (especially with Class 4 impact resistance).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How it handles Utah weather: Quality architectural shingles resist high winds and handle dramatic freeze-thaw cycles well. Class 4 shingles can also qualify you for a 20% to 30% discount on homeowners insurance from many Wasatch Front carriers.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Best for: Standard suburban homes looking for the best balance of cost, curb appeal, and local contractor availability. (Roughly 60% of installations in Utah counties are architectural asphalt).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              5,
              6
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "2. Standing-Seam Metal Roofing (Best for Longevity & Snow)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan: 40\u201370+ years.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How it handles Utah weather: Snow naturally slides off the smooth surface, preventing dangerous snow loads and ice dams. Reflective metal also keeps homes cooler during scorching 100\u00b0F+ summer days and shrugs off high winds and UV degradation.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3,
              4,
              7
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Best for: Mountain properties, heavy snow country (like upper Weber or Davis counties), and long-term \"forever\" homes. Note: Snow guards are usually required to keep sheets of snow from sliding off onto entryways.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              8
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "3. Stone-Coated Steel (Best Hybrid Look & Strength)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan: 50+ years."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How it handles Utah weather: Gives you the heavy-duty metal strength and hail/fire resistance while mimicking the textured appearance of traditional shingles or tile."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Best for: Homeowners who want metal-grade resilience without the modern industrial look of standing-seam panels.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "4. Concrete Tile (Best for Specific Aesthetics)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Lifespan: 50+ years.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "How it handles Utah weather: Concrete handles freeze-thaw cycles better than clay tile, though proper underlayment is critical to keep melting snow from leaking.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Best for: Heavier custom homes built to support the structural weight, keeping in mind that Utah is an active seismic zone where tiles can occasionally shift during earthquakes.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
